[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162992"] :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{x-2y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+x+a\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =4{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+4\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+x+(-2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+2=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102/selection/e819017e19dccac106b8bdd7a345917915a57ae6ae3646d73f366105b31b91f7" :answer/id #uuid "a9a7dc7a-dfc6-512d-b7d9-0f9fff310c31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102/selection/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e" :answer/id #uuid "5b4da1ac-a9a9-5994-bf6d-f8dff33e935b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102/selection/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11" :answer/id #uuid "77d07acf-85bf-569d-8a97-b30f8e63c624" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102/selection/b306ad6a3af7dc043f4c3e03da26a15440a9e9b19389783b2e1203bad6e37573" :answer/id #uuid "83af7234-dfd4-5042-a19c-3de99d6cada7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102/selection/51e5d6d80d0167bf853a20f864544b395aab1aa280e00fbc15a90a3d607b21c9" :answer/id #uuid "f3296e2c-c848-58b7-95ad-1c12a1a0c525" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b44c59d0-6099-5fc7-9331-ce2d8d5038d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163102/selection/b306ad6a3af7dc043f4c3e03da26a15440a9e9b19389783b2e1203bad6e37573" :answer-field/choices ["q-163102/selection/e819017e19dccac106b8bdd7a345917915a57ae6ae3646d73f366105b31b91f7" "q-163102/selection/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e" "q-163102/selection/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11" "q-163102/selection/b306ad6a3af7dc043f4c3e03da26a15440a9e9b19389783b2e1203bad6e37573" "q-163102/selection/51e5d6d80d0167bf853a20f864544b395aab1aa280e00fbc15a90a3d607b21c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163102" :question/id #uuid "9ab3d7fc-b7a1-5808-8e28-249275bd1920" :question/math-academy-id "q-163102" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y & \\frac{x}{y}+x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}+1 & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n2+(-1)+(x-1)=x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163102/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163102"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104/selection/745af980d12397d3856556d55d6daaeacc66ba29fc98bcf8d6be4d22981e40fe" :answer/id #uuid "bb51c752-7d86-544c-984c-4af5aebf8f7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104/selection/c3104ff4fd1d4cd331c6a47a13130e95fc0dd95e882d63a50d9f91e6183998b8" :answer/id #uuid "8f6a96e6-e36e-5ad4-931e-6fd09afafc5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104/selection/3a31ca3153be775416fd184d00cd1bc9792bb8537ccb79c1af44608f0d09455d" :answer/id #uuid "766f088e-6755-546a-8b90-0f2091cf54e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104/selection/f942985d646228ebcfa1892460f5950f9035ace2f9fcc38b6495a60ae632b124" :answer/id #uuid "e9064f50-1adb-530d-af0a-5fa813637048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104/selection/ebb95e1a4ce30d95a498ba3efbf7558fb3c5c1bc9219c3a2b06a2963d4938b5c" :answer/id #uuid "b9bb9cf0-bf58-5f4e-8613-6daf2fbcfec3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d147cf49-4a6e-58d0-80ad-a632a32ee0fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163104/selection/c3104ff4fd1d4cd331c6a47a13130e95fc0dd95e882d63a50d9f91e6183998b8" :answer-field/choices ["q-163104/selection/745af980d12397d3856556d55d6daaeacc66ba29fc98bcf8d6be4d22981e40fe" "q-163104/selection/c3104ff4fd1d4cd331c6a47a13130e95fc0dd95e882d63a50d9f91e6183998b8" "q-163104/selection/3a31ca3153be775416fd184d00cd1bc9792bb8537ccb79c1af44608f0d09455d" "q-163104/selection/f942985d646228ebcfa1892460f5950f9035ace2f9fcc38b6495a60ae632b124" "q-163104/selection/ebb95e1a4ce30d95a498ba3efbf7558fb3c5c1bc9219c3a2b06a2963d4938b5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163104" :question/id #uuid "57b97338-5c6b-54b7-875a-013dc5343be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163104" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y)^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & ∗ \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3(x-y)^{2} & -3(x-y)^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}6(x-y) & -6(x-y) \\\\ -6(x-y) & 6(x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\nx+6+(y-1)=x+y+5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163104/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163104"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "5ab8839d-1cdb-541f-b23d-b3e16b281944" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "2a94be21-1570-5af0-8dd8-59bd07104982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2f213f94-385f-5de6-8ef4-e2779f2cd60f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "0870d895-57b5-5b75-a9f5-27e66c95f53d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "e1c0697a-9a0d-5886-9e09-0567d709f8db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "39951829-8446-5d44-9a66-10d2a5dffa82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163111/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-163111/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-163111/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-163111/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-163111/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-163111/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163111" :question/id #uuid "bc80676d-e8a8-589e-9687-c0d899f9a26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163111" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y+1),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x-y+1}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{x-y+1} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1xy+(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-163111/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163111"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-164611"] :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & ≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(4) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
